1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,390
This cruise ship cost about four

2
00:00:01,440 --> 00:00:03,950
hundred and fifty million euros to build.

3
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:06,690
Getting rid of her cost
more than three times that.

4
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:08,030
Not to repair her.

5
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:09,390
Not to sail her again.

6
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:12,170
Just to get her off a rock,
and cut her up for scrap.

7
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:16,330
To do it, engineers had to roll a
ship almost three hundred metres

8
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:20,350
long back upright, in one piece,
without breaking her in half.

9
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:23,250
No ship that big had ever
been lifted like this.

10
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:25,290
But why did a modern cruise ship end

11
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:27,050
up on that rock in the first place?

12
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:29,210
To find out, we have to
go back to one night.

13
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:30,710
A calm sea.

14
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:32,530
A ship full of people at dinner.

15
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:35,410
And a mistake that cost thirty-two lives.

16
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:37,990
The ship was called the Costa Concordia.

17
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:40,150
Built in Italy in two thousand and six,

18
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:43,110
she was about two hundred
and ninety metres long,

19
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:46,290
with room for more than three
thousand seven hundred passengers.

20
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:49,230
Pools, theatres, restaurants, a spa.

21
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:51,250
Basically a floating city.

22
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:54,970
That night, she had just left
the port of Civitavecchia,

23
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:58,530
near Rome, on a week-long
cruise around the Mediterranean.

24
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:01,910
On board were three
thousand two hundred and six

25
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:04,870
passengers and one thousand
and twenty-three crew.

26
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:06,320
The weather was good.

27
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:07,750
The sea was calm.

28
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:10,110
The next stop was Savona, in the north.

29
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:13,510
And the route went past a
small island called Giglio.

30
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:15,890
Now, a big ship normally passes an island

31
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:17,930
like that with plenty of room to spare.

32
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:20,110
But that night, the ship was taken

33
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:22,150
much closer to the coast than usual.

34
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:25,250
The plan was to pass about
half a mile from the shore.

35
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:25,970
At night.

36
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:28,370
In an area with very few lights.

37
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:29,990
And she was moving fast.

38
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:31,570
About sixteen knots.

39
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:34,150
That's roughly thirty kilometres an hour,

40
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:36,650
for a ship that weighs
as much as a small town.

41
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:39,510
Italy's official investigators later went

42
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:41,510
through everything that
happened on the bridge.

43
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:43,970
They wrote that the close
pass had been planned

44
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:46,550
on a chart that wasn't
detailed enough for it.

45
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:49,510
That people who weren't part of
the watch were on the bridge.

46
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:51,350
And that there was a phone call that

47
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:53,270
had nothing to do with navigation.

48
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:56,570
They also said something
about the officers on duty.

49
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:01,090
Nobody seemed to push the captain
to slow down or turn away in time.

50
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:04,870
By the time the danger was
clear, it was too late.

51
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:07,010
At nine forty-five and seven seconds,

52
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:10,850
the Costa Concordia hit a group of
rocks off Giglio called the Scole.

53
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:13,850
The rock tore a gash in
the left side of the hull,

54
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:17,030
fifty-three metres long,
below the waterline.

55
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:19,170
To understand why that was so bad,

56
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:21,770
you need to know how a ship
stays afloat when it's damaged.

57
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:26,130
The hull is split into watertight
compartments, like an ice cube tray.

58
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:29,190
If one or two flood, the
ship should still float.

59
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:32,330
The Concordia's gash opened
five of them at once.

60
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:34,890
And those five held the heart of the ship.

61
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:36,280
The engine room.

62
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:37,690
The propulsion motors.

63
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:39,590
All six of the main generators.

64
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:42,930
So within moments, the ship
lost her engines and her power.

65
00:02:43,260 --> 00:02:45,710
The emergency generator
kept cutting in and out.

66
00:02:45,980 --> 00:02:48,390
And the rudder was stuck,
turned hard to one side.

67
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:51,110
A ship with more than four
thousand people on board

68
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:54,450
was now drifting in the
dark, with water rushing in.

69
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:57,490
And here's the part that
still shocks people.

70
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:01,210
For more than an hour, the ship
did not order everyone to leave.

71
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:04,710
At first, the coast guard didn't
even hear it from the ship.

72
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:06,930
Someone on shore raised the alarm,

73
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:10,190
and the rescue started at
sixteen minutes past ten.

74
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:14,830
By twenty-seven minutes past ten,
the water had reached the deck

75
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:18,550
that's supposed to stay dry, the
top of the watertight compartments.

76
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:23,970
And once that happens, the sea can spill
from one compartment into the next,

77
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:27,090
like water tipping over the
top of an ice cube tray.

78
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:31,410
The official distress call only
came at twenty-two thirty-eight.

79
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:35,030
And the order to abandon
ship came at five to eleven.

80
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:37,530
That's more than an hour after the rocks.

81
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:40,950
And before it, the general
emergency alarm that should

82
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,550
have warned everyone in time
was not sounded straight away.

83
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:48,150
By then, the ship had
already started to lean.

84
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:50,410
There was one piece of luck.

85
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:52,870
With the rudder jammed,
the drifting ship turned

86
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:55,950
around by herself and drifted
back toward the island.

87
00:03:56,240 --> 00:03:58,690
At around eleven o'clock,
she ran aground on a rock

88
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:02,050
shelf near Giglio's little
harbour, just off the shore.

89
00:04:02,220 --> 00:04:05,610
If she had sunk in open water,
it could have been much worse.

90
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:08,550
The first lifeboats went
down at five to eleven,

91
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:11,010
and reached the shore a
quarter of an hour later.

92
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:12,950
But the ship kept leaning.

93
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:14,850
Forty degrees around midnight.

94
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:16,610
Later, almost eighty.

95
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:18,530
On a ship leaning that far,

96
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:21,330
lifeboats on the high side
can't be lowered at all.

97
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:22,770
Corridors become walls.

98
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:24,350
Stairs become ladders.

99
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:27,230
People climbed down rope
ladders on the side of the hull,

100
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:31,330
waited on deck, or jumped into
the sea and swam for the rocks.

101
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:35,330
Twenty-five patrol boats,
fourteen other vessels,

102
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:38,290
four tugs and eight
helicopters joined the rescue.

103
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:40,830
The people of Giglio opened their homes,

104
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:43,490
their churches and their
school to the survivors.

105
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:44,940
And the captain?

106
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:48,430
He left the ship while hundreds
of people were still on board.

107
00:04:48,620 --> 00:04:51,870
A coast guard officer famously
ordered him, by phone, to go back.

108
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:53,230
He didn't.

109
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:54,970
The first rescue finished at seventeen

110
00:04:55,020 --> 00:04:56,810
minutes past six the next morning.

111
00:04:57,140 --> 00:04:58,940
Four thousand one hundred and

112
00:04:58,990 --> 00:05:00,830
ninety-four people were saved that night.

113
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:03,090
Three more were found
alive two days later.

114
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:06,550
But thirty-two people
died, passengers and crew.

115
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:09,170
Then came a different kind of problem.

116
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:13,710
The Concordia was now lying on
her side, on a sloping rock shelf,

117
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:16,830
right next to a small island
in a protected marine area.

118
00:05:17,020 --> 00:05:20,090
More than a hundred thousand
tonnes of ship, full of fuel,

119
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:24,090
food, furniture and everything
the passengers had left behind.

120
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:26,630
First, salvors pumped out the fuel.

121
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:29,550
More than two thousand cubic
metres of oil were removed

122
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:32,630
in the first months, before
it could leak into the sea.

123
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:34,950
Then came the big question.

124
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:36,770
What do you do with the ship?

125
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:38,270
The easy answer would have been to

126
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:41,090
cut her up right there, piece by piece.

127
00:05:41,260 --> 00:05:44,810
But that would have taken
years, right next to the island,

128
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:47,750
with pieces of wreck and
dirt falling into the sea.

129
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:50,550
So the owners chose the harder plan.

130
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,730
Turn the whole ship upright, float
her, and tow her away in one piece.

131
00:05:56,160 --> 00:05:58,350
The job went to two
companies working together.

132
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:02,370
Titan Salvage, from the United
States, and Micoperi, from Italy.

133
00:06:02,420 --> 00:06:06,390
Hundreds of workers and divers
from all over the world.

134
00:06:06,860 --> 00:06:09,250
And the trick they used has a name.

135
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:10,800
Parbuckling.

136
00:06:11,180 --> 00:06:12,790
Parbuckling means rolling something

137
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:15,370
upright by pulling it around its own side.

138
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:17,710
Like rolling a barrel
up a ramp with ropes.

139
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:20,130
Except this barrel was a cruise ship.

140
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:22,050
But first, they had to stop her from

141
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:24,490
sliding off the rock
shelf into deep water.

142
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:28,310
So divers filled huge bags with
cement and packed them under the hull.

143
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:32,170
And they drilled into the granite
seabed and built six giant

144
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:35,870
steel platforms next to the ship,
to catch her when she turned.

145
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,630
Then they welded enormous
steel boxes, called sponsons,

146
00:06:39,680 --> 00:06:41,830
onto the side that was
sticking out of the water.

147
00:06:42,100 --> 00:06:43,890
Some of them were taller than a house.

148
00:06:43,940 --> 00:06:46,090
And they ran thick chains under the hull,

149
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:50,750
linked to steel cables and powerful
jacks, all anchored to the seabed.

150
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:53,990
On the sixteenth of
September twenty thirteen,

151
00:06:54,160 --> 00:06:56,930
at nine in the morning,
they started pulling.

152
00:06:57,240 --> 00:06:58,750
At first, nothing moved.

153
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:00,830
The ship had been lying
in the same spot for

154
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:03,390
twenty months, and she
was stuck on the rocks.

155
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:05,730
The jacks pulled harder and harder, until,

156
00:07:05,780 --> 00:07:08,510
with a groan of steel,
she finally came free.

157
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:11,270
Then, very slowly, she started to turn.

158
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:12,570
All day.

159
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:13,620
All night.

160
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:17,910
Partway up, the engineers let
seawater flood into the steel boxes.

161
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:20,670
Now the weight of that water
did the rest of the work,

162
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:23,150
pulling the ship the
rest of the way round.

163
00:07:23,520 --> 00:07:26,290
At four in the morning on
the seventeenth of September,

164
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:28,730
about nineteen hours after they started,

165
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:31,670
the Costa Concordia was sitting
upright on the platforms.

166
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:33,450
The side that had been underwater for

167
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:36,550
twenty months was brown,
crushed and twisted.

168
00:07:36,980 --> 00:07:38,330
But she still couldn't float.

169
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:41,470
So the salvors welded more
sponsons onto the other side.

170
00:07:41,740 --> 00:07:42,740
Thirty in total.

171
00:07:42,840 --> 00:07:46,410
Then, in July twenty fourteen,
they pushed the water out of them

172
00:07:46,460 --> 00:07:50,110
with compressed air, and the ship
slowly rose off the platforms.

173
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:54,630
On the twenty-third of July,
tugs started towing her north.

174
00:07:54,980 --> 00:07:58,810
About two hundred nautical miles,
in four days, at walking speed.

175
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:01,430
On the twenty-seventh,
she arrived in Genoa.

176
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:04,730
There, it took around three
years to take her apart.

177
00:08:05,020 --> 00:08:07,210
About ninety per cent of
the ship was recycled.

178
00:08:07,340 --> 00:08:09,310
So how much did all of this cost?

179
00:08:09,540 --> 00:08:12,510
Costa's chief executive
said the whole operation,

180
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:16,750
from the reef to Genoa, would cost
around one and a half billion euros.

181
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:18,270
Roughly two billion dollars.

182
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:21,110
Much more than it cost to build
the ship in the first place.

183
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:22,650
And it wasn't only money.

184
00:08:22,880 --> 00:08:25,270
A diver working on the salvage also died.

185
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:29,410
The captain, Francesco Schettino,
was convicted of manslaughter,

186
00:08:29,620 --> 00:08:32,250
of causing the shipwreck,
and of abandoning the ship.

187
00:08:32,540 --> 00:08:34,490
He was sentenced to
sixteen years in prison.

188
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:37,590
In May twenty seventeen,
Italy's highest court

189
00:08:37,640 --> 00:08:40,390
upheld that sentence, and he went to jail.

190
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:43,950
The official report didn't
only blame one man, though.

191
00:08:44,160 --> 00:08:46,010
It said the flooding was far beyond

192
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:47,770
what the ship was designed to survive.

193
00:08:47,820 --> 00:08:49,810
That the rules at the time didn't require

194
00:08:49,860 --> 00:08:51,990
the ship to have sensors
to detect flooding.

195
00:08:52,220 --> 00:08:54,590
And that the bridge team
didn't work as a team.

196
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:56,550
So let's go back to that night.

197
00:08:57,360 --> 00:08:58,360
Nine forty-five.

198
00:08:58,760 --> 00:08:59,750
A calm sea.

199
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:03,510
A ship that was sailing too
close, too fast, in the dark.

200
00:09:03,940 --> 00:09:04,940
One rock.

201
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:06,550
A fifty-three metre gash.

202
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:08,830
An hour before anyone was told to leave.

203
00:09:09,160 --> 00:09:10,730
Thirty-two people lost.

204
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:13,250
Two years of engineering
to lift what was left.

205
00:09:13,380 --> 00:09:15,170
And around two billion dollars.

206
00:09:15,220 --> 00:09:17,970
All because of a few
minutes on the bridge.

207
00:09:18,340 --> 00:09:21,010
And if you want to see another
ship that lost all her power

208
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:24,770
at the worst possible moment,
that story is right here.
